    {
      "id": "claim_afd2669a22e54c6385b5a8a7aa58985f",
      "statement": "Default values can be specified using @DefaultValue on constructor parameters and record components. Property placeholders in the annotation’s String value are replaced and the conversion service is applied to coerce it to the target type of a missing property.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://upstream/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/features/external-config.adoc#L850-L951",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4aa5a82c6e9a60c26b06de4001cb78d601425906f6b6d109d9dc2f333d5916d4:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d5e5716b41164899b57a293f03280467",
      "statement": "In the MyProperties example above, you can see the nested Security class uses @DefaultValue(\"USER\") for the roles parameter. This means that if security properties are defined, but roles is not, the default of \"USER\" will be bound.",
